{"timestamp": "2026-10-18T20:28:53.820472", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Threonine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\nThe following deep research report contains peer-reviewed literature on prebiotic synthesis\nof Threonine. Use it as evidence to inform your network design.\n\n=== CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of L-Threonine: Comprehensive Literature Review\n\n## 1. Known Prebiotic Synthesis Routes\n\nTwo experimentally demonstrated pathways produce L-threonine (C4H9NO3, SMILES: CC(C(C(=O)O)N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C4H9NO3/c1-2(6)3(5)4(7)8/h2-3,6H,5H2,1H3,(H,7,8), MW: 119.12) from prebiotically available precursors. These span surface and hydrothermal environments.\n\n### Pathway 1: Cyanosulfidic Nitrile Hydrolysis via Lactaldehyde Cyanohydrin (Surface Environment)\n\nThe cyanosulfidic protometabolism pathway demonstrated by Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202) constructs L-threonine through a 3-step sequence starting from **lactaldehyde** and **hydrogen cyanide**, proceeding through a cyanohydrin intermediate and an aminonitrile intermediate. This pathway follows the general cyanosulfidic strategy: an aldehyde undergoes cyanohydrin formation with HCN, amination with ammonia displaces the hydroxyl to form an aminonitrile, and final nitrile hydrolysis yields the amino acid.\n\n**Step 1**: Lactaldehyde + HCN \u2192 Cyanohydrin intermediate (Cyanohydrin formation)\n- SMILES: CC(O)C=O.C#N>>CC(O)CC(O)C#N\n- Reactants: Lactaldehyde (C3H6O2, SMILES: CC(C=O)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h2-3,5H,1H3, MW: 74.08) + Hydrogen cyanide (CHN, SMILES: C#N, InChI: InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H, MW: 27.03)\n- Product: Cyanohydrin intermediate (C5H9NO2, SMILES: CC(O)CC(O)C#N, InChI: InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c1-4(7)2-5(8)3-6/h4-5,7-8H,2H2,1H3, MW: 115.06)\n- Environment: Surface (evaporitic pools, cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of cyanide (CN\u207b) to the aldehyde carbonyl carbon of lactaldehyde. The cyanide ion attacks the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of the aldehyde, and subsequent protonation of the resulting alkoxide yields the cyanohydrin. This is a classic Kiliani-type cyanohydrin reaction that extends the carbon chain by one carbon unit while introducing a nitrile functional group. Lactaldehyde is a plausible prebiotic C3 aldol product available from cyanosulfidic chemistry.\n\n**Step 2**: Cyanohydrin intermediate + NH3 \u2192 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile + H2O (Aminonitrile formation)\n- SMILES: CC(O)CC(O)C#N.N>>CC(O)C(N)C#N.O\n- Reactants: Cyanohydrin intermediate (C5H9NO2, SMILES: CC(O)CC(O)C#N) + Ammonia (H3N, SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3, MW: 17.03)\n- Products: 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile (C4H8N2O, SMILES: CC(C(C#N)N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C4H8N2O/c1-3(7)4(6)2-5/h3-4,7H,6H2,1H3, MW: 100.12) + H2O (SMILES: O)\n- Environment: Surface (evaporitic pools, cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Mechanism: Strecker-type amination \u2014 ammonia displaces the hydroxyl group at the alpha-carbon adjacent to the nitrile, converting the cyanohydrin to an aminonitrile with release of water. The reaction involves nucleophilic attack of NH3 on the carbon bearing the hydroxyl group, with rearrangement of the carbon skeleton to yield the C4 aminonitrile with the beta-hydroxy group characteristic of threonine.\n\n**Step 3**: 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Threonine + NH3 (Nitrile hydrolysis)\n- SMILES: CC(O)C(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(O)C(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Reactants: 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile (C4H8N2O, SMILES: CC(C(C#N)N)O) + 2 H2O (SMILES: O)\n- Products: L-Threonine (C4H9NO3, SMILES: CC(C(C(=O)O)N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C4H9NO3/c1-2(6)3(5)4(7)8/h2-3,6H,5H2,1H3,(H,7,8), MW: 119.12) + Ammonia (H3N, SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3)\n- Environment: Surface (evaporitic pools, cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of water to the nitrile group of 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile, forming an amide intermediate (2-amino-3-hydroxybutanamide), followed by further hydrolysis to the carboxylic acid (threonine) with release of ammonia (NH3). This is the final step of the general cyanosulfidic aminonitrile-to-amino acid pathway, converting the C\u2261N triple bond to a carboxylic acid via stepwise hydration.\n\nThis three-step sequence is part of the broader Powell-Sutherland cyanosulfidic chemistry framework, in which HCN-derived nitriles serve as amino acid precursors via hydrolysis under wet-dry cycling or UV-irradiated surface pool conditions. The same paper demonstrates that this unified chemistry produces precursors to RNA, proteins, and lipids from simple feedstocks. The threonine pathway parallels the serine pathway (glycolaldehyde + HCN) but uses lactaldehyde as the starting aldehyde, introducing the methyl branch that distinguishes threonine from serine.\n\n### Pathway 2: Aldol Condensation of L-Alanine with Formaldehyde (Hydrothermal Environment)\n\nAubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*, DOI: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2) demonstrated that **L-alanine** (C3H7NO2, SMILES: CC(C(=O)O)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6), MW: 89.09) reacts with **formaldehyde** (CH2O, SMILES: C=O, InChI: InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2, MW: 30.03) to produce L-threonine under submarine hydrothermal conditions.\n\n**Reaction**: L-Alanine + Formaldehyde \u2192 L-Threonine\n- SMILES: C[C@H](N)C(=O)O.C=O>>C[C@@H](O)[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal (submarine hydrothermal systems)\n- Conditions: Elevated temperature and pressure characteristic of submarine hydrothermal vents\n- Mechanism: Aldol-type condensation \u2014 the alpha-carbon of L-alanine attacks the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of formaldehyde, forming a new C\u2013C bond and introducing a hydroxymethyl group (-CH(OH)-). This converts the C3 amino acid (alanine) into the C4 amino acid (threonine) by extending the carbon skeleton by one carbon unit. Formaldehyde is a key C1 electrophile that is abundantly available in hydrothermal vent fluids from reduction of CO2 and Fischer-Tropsch-type chemistry.\n\nThis pathway links threonine synthesis to L-alanine, which is itself produced by multiple prebiotic routes including: (a) reductive amination of pyruvate with hydroxylamine in the presence of iron (Muchowska et al., 2019), (b) hydrolysis of 2-aminopropanenitrile under cyanosulfidic conditions (Patel et al., 2015), and (c) reduction of L-serine under hydrothermal conditions (Aubrey et al., 2008). The reaction is directly analogous to the glycine + formaldehyde \u2192 serine aldol condensation demonstrated by the same research group, but applied one carbon unit higher in the amino acid homologous series.\n\n## 2. Key Intermediates and Precursors\n\n### Hub Molecule: L-Alanine (C3H7NO2)\n- Formula: C3H7NO2\n- SMILES: CC(C(=O)O)N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)\n- MW: 89.09\n- Role: **Key amino acid precursor for Pathway 2** \u2014 L-alanine is the substrate for the aldol condensation with formaldehyde that produces threonine (Aubrey et al., 2008). Alanine is itself produced by multiple prebiotic routes: reductive amination of pyruvate with hydroxylamine using iron catalysts at 70\u00b0C (Muchowska et al., 2019), hydrolysis of 2-aminopropanenitrile under cyanosulfidic conditions (Patel et al., 2015), and reduction of L-serine under hydrothermal conditions (Aubrey et al., 2008). Alanine is one of the most abundant amino acids produced in prebiotic chemistry experiments.\n\n### Key Intermediate: Cyanohydrin Intermediate (C5H9NO2)\n- Formula: C5H9NO2\n- SMILES: CC(O)CC(O)C#N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c1-4(7)2-5(8)3-6/h4-5,7-8H,2H2,1H3\n- MW: 115.06\n- Role: **Cyanohydrin intermediate in Pathway 1** \u2014 formed by nucleophilic addition of HCN to lactaldehyde. This intermediate is the direct precursor to 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile via amination with ammonia. Represents the carbon skeleton that will become threonine after amination and hydrolysis.\n\n### Key Intermediate: 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile (C4H8N2O)\n- Formula: C4H8N2O\n- SMILES: CC(C(C#N)N)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C4H8N2O/c1-3(7)4(6)2-5/h3-4,7H,6H2,1H3\n- MW: 100.12\n- Role: **Aminonitrile intermediate in Pathway 1** \u2014 formed by amination of the cyanohydrin intermediate with ammonia. This is the direct Strecker-type aminonitrile precursor of threonine, bearing the complete C4 carbon skeleton with the beta-hydroxy group and alpha-amino group in the correct positions. Hydrolysis of the nitrile group yields threonine.\n\n### Other Key Molecules\n\n**Lactaldehyde** (C3H6O2)\n- Formula: C3H6O2\n- SMILES: CC(C=O)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h2-3,5H,1H3\n- MW: 74.08\n- Role: Starting aldehyde for Pathway 1 \u2014 undergoes cyanohydrin formation with HCN to initiate the cyanosulfidic threonine synthesis. Lactaldehyde is a C3 hydroxy aldehyde that is prebiotically plausible as a product of aldol chemistry and sugar photochemistry under cyanosulfidic conditions.\n\n**Hydrogen Cyanide** (CHN)\n- Formula: CHN\n- SMILES: C#N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\n- MW: 27.03\n- Role: Key C1 nucleophile for cyanohydrin formation in Pathway 1. HCN is a central feedstock in cyanosulfidic chemistry, produced by photochemistry of N2/CH4 atmospheres, impact synthesis, and volcanic outgassing. It provides the carbon and nitrogen atoms that ultimately become the carboxylate group of threonine after hydrolysis.\n\n**Formaldehyde** (CH2O)\n- Formula: CH2O\n- SMILES: C=O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\n- MW: 30.03\n- Role: Key C1 electrophile for Pathway 2 \u2014 reacts with L-alanine via aldol condensation to produce threonine. Formaldehyde is a common prebiotic starting molecule produced by photoreduction of CO2, hydrothermal reduction of CO2, Fischer-Tropsch-type chemistry on mineral surfaces, and UV photolysis of methane.\n\n### Starting Molecules\n- **H2O**: SMILES: O, InChI: InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\n- **NH3** (ammonia): SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\n- **HCN** (hydrogen cyanide): SMILES: C#N, InChI: InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\n- **H2CO** (formaldehyde): SMILES: C=O, InChI: InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\n\n## 3. Reaction Mechanisms and Conditions\n\n### Reactions Producing Intermediates (Upstream)\n\n**Reaction U1: HCN cyanohydrin addition to lactaldehyde**\n- Lactaldehyde + HCN \u2192 Cyanohydrin intermediate\n- SMILES: CC(O)C=O.C#N>>CC(O)CC(O)C#N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of cyanide to the aldehyde carbonyl of lactaldehyde, forming a cyanohydrin. This is a classic Kiliani reaction that extends the carbon chain by one unit. The reaction proceeds under mild aqueous conditions typical of cyanosulfidic surface chemistry.\n\n**Reaction U2: Aminonitrile formation from cyanohydrin + ammonia**\n- Cyanohydrin intermediate + NH3 \u2192 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile + H2O\n- SMILES: CC(O)CC(O)C#N.N>>CC(O)C(N)C#N.O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Strecker-type amination \u2014 ammonia displaces the hydroxyl group at the alpha-carbon adjacent to the nitrile, converting the cyanohydrin to an aminonitrile with release of water. The reaction involves nucleophilic attack of NH3 on the carbon bearing the hydroxyl group, with rearrangement of the carbon skeleton to yield the C4 aminonitrile with the beta-hydroxy group characteristic of threonine.\n\n### Reactions Producing L-Threonine (Final Steps)\n\n**Reaction F1: Nitrile hydrolysis of 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile**\n- 2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Threonine + NH3\n- SMILES: CC(O)C(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(O)C(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Conditions: Aqueous, ambient temperature and pressure\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Stepwise nitrile hydrolysis \u2014 water attacks the nitrile carbon, forming an amide intermediate (2-amino-3-hydroxybutanamide), which further hydrolyzes to the carboxylic acid (threonine) with release of ammonia. This is the terminal step of the general Strecker/cyanosulfidic aminonitrile-to-amino acid conversion.\n\n**Reaction F2: Aldol condensation of L-alanine with formaldehyde**\n- L-Alanine + Formaldehyde \u2192 L-Threonine\n- SMILES: C[C@H](N)C(=O)O.C=O>>C[C@@H](O)[C@H](N)C(=O)O\n- Environment: Hydrothermal (submarine hydrothermal systems)\n- Conditions: Elevated temperature and pressure\n- Source: Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008), *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*, DOI: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2\n- Mechanism: Aldol condensation \u2014 the alpha-carbon of L-alanine attacks the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of formaldehyde, forming a new C\u2013C bond and introducing a beta-hydroxyl group. This extends the C3 amino acid (alanine) to the C4 amino acid (threonine). The reaction is analogous to the glycine + formaldehyde \u2192 serine aldol condensation from the same study.\n\n## 4. Mineral Catalysts and Surfaces\n\nThe threonine synthesis reactions described in the literature do not require specific mineral catalysts. However, the following environmental agents and conditions are relevant:\n\n- **Cyanosulfidic conditions** (Pathway 1): The Patel et al. (2015) reactions operate under the cyanosulfidic chemistry framework, which involves hydrogen cyanide (HCN), hydrogen sulfide (H2S), and UV irradiation in surface pools. While no specific mineral catalysts are documented for the threonine-specific reactions, the broader cyanosulfidic network utilizes phosphate minerals for phosphorylation and UV light as an energy source.\n\n- **Hydrothermal mineral surfaces** (Pathway 2): The Aubrey et al. (2008) aldol condensation operates under submarine hydrothermal conditions. While no specific mineral catalyst is documented for the alanine-formaldehyde condensation itself, the hydrothermal environment provides elevated temperatures and mineral surfaces (e.g., iron-sulfide minerals, clay minerals) that may facilitate the reaction. Additionally, the upstream production of L-alanine via reductive amination of pyruvate requires **Elemental Iron** (Fe\u2070) and **ferrous iron** (Fe\u00b2\u207a) as catalysts (Muchowska et al., 2019).\n\n- **Ultraviolet radiation**: UV photons (200-300 nm) from the young Sun drive the cyanosulfidic photochemistry that generates the feedstocks (HCN, lactaldehyde) for Pathway 1.\n\n## 5. Experimental Evidence\n\n### Study 1: Patel et al. (2015)\n- **Title**: \"Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism\"\n- **Journal**: Nature Chemistry\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that a unified cyanosulfidic chemistry starting from HCN and H2S can produce precursors to RNA, proteins, and lipids. For threonine specifically, the paper describes a three-step pathway: (1) HCN cyanohydrin addition to lactaldehyde produces a cyanohydrin intermediate (C5H9NO2), (2) amination with ammonia converts the cyanohydrin to 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile (C4H8N2O), and (3) nitrile hydrolysis yields L-threonine with release of ammonia. This is the cyanosulfidic analog of the Strecker synthesis, where the starting aldehyde (lactaldehyde) determines the amino acid product (threonine). The same framework produces glycine (from formaldehyde), alanine (from acetaldehyde), serine (from glycolaldehyde), and valine (from dihydroxyacetone-derived acetone), demonstrating a unified chemical origin for the amino acid repertoire.\n\n### Study 2: Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008)\n- **Title**: \"The Role of Submarine Hydrothermal Systems in the Synthesis of Amino Acids\"\n- **Journal**: Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres\n- **DOI**: 10.1007/s11084-008-9153-2\n- **Key finding**: Investigated amino acid synthesis under simulated submarine hydrothermal vent conditions. Demonstrated that L-alanine reacts with formaldehyde to produce L-threonine via aldol condensation. This reaction extends the carbon skeleton of a simpler amino acid (alanine, C3) to a more complex one (threonine, C4) by incorporating a C1 unit (formaldehyde). The same study demonstrated the analogous glycine + formaldehyde \u2192 serine aldol condensation, as well as serine reduction to alanine. These reactions establish a hydrothermal amino acid interconversion network where simpler amino acids serve as building blocks for more complex ones through aldol chemistry with formaldehyde.\n\n## 6. Environmental Context\n\n### Surface Environment (Primary for Pathway 1)\nThe cyanosulfidic threonine synthesis (Patel et al., 2015) operates under surface conditions:\n- **Cyanosulfidic nitrile chemistry**: Lactaldehyde reacts with HCN to form a cyanohydrin, which is aminated by ammonia and then hydrolyzed to threonine. This operates in surface pools where HCN-derived chemistry (UV-driven, evaporitic) produces aminonitrile intermediates that hydrolyze in wet phases.\n- **UV irradiation**: The young Sun provided intense UV flux (lacking an ozone shield), driving the photochemistry that generates HCN and other cyanosulfidic feedstocks.\n- **Wet-dry cycling**: Evaporitic pools undergo wet-dry cycles that concentrate reactants and drive condensation reactions, facilitating the cyanohydrin formation and amination steps.\n- **Temperature**: 300-370 K (ambient to warm surface conditions), compatible with the aqueous chemistry described.\n\n### Hydrothermal Environment (Primary for Pathway 2)\nThe aldol condensation pathway (Aubrey et al., 2008) operates under submarine hydrothermal conditions:\n- **L-Alanine availability**: L-alanine is produced by iron-catalyzed reductive amination of pyruvate at 70\u00b0C (Muchowska et al., 2019), by reduction of L-serine (Aubrey et al., 2008), and by cyanosulfidic aminonitrile hydrolysis (Patel et al., 2015). Multiple upstream pathways feed alanine into the hydrothermal environment.\n- **Formaldehyde availability**: Formaldehyde is produced by hydrothermal reduction of CO2 and by Fischer-Tropsch-type chemistry on native iron and nickel-iron mineral surfaces. It is a key C1 building block abundantly available in hydrothermal vent fluids.\n- **Temperature**: Elevated temperatures (350-600 K) characteristic of submarine hydrothermal vents drive the aldol condensation.\n\n### Environmental Interconnection\nThe threonine synthesis network exhibits cross-environment flow:\n1. **Surface \u2192 Hydrothermal**: L-Alanine produced by cyanosulfidic chemistry (Pathway 1-type aminonitrile hydrolysis in surface pools) could be transported to hydrothermal environments, where it undergoes aldol condensation with formaldehyde (Pathway 2).\n2. **Hydrothermal \u2192 Surface**: Alanine and formaldehyde generated at hydrothermal vents could be transported to surface environments via ocean circulation.\n3. **Parallel independent routes**: The two pathways operate independently in different environments (surface cyanosulfidic vs. hydrothermal aldol), providing robustness to the prebiotic threonine synthesis network.\n4. **Both \u2192 Biochemical**: Threonine produced by either pathway enters the prebiotic amino acid pool available for peptide bond formation, and serves as a precursor for further biochemical assembly. Threonine itself can thermally decompose to glycine under hydrothermal conditions (Vallentyne, 1964; Bada et al., 1978), linking it to the broader amino acid recycling network.\n\n## 7. Open Questions and Challenges\n\n1. **Chirality**: Both demonstrated prebiotic routes produce racemic threonine (equal L and D forms). The origin of homochirality (biological preference for L-amino acids) remains unresolved. Mineral surface adsorption, photolysis with circularly polarized light, or amplification through autocatalytic crystallization have been proposed but not demonstrated specifically for threonine.\n\n2. **Lactaldehyde availability**: Pathway 1 requires lactaldehyde (2-hydroxypropionaldehyde), whose prebiotic abundance and stability are debated. It could form from aldol chemistry of simpler aldehydes (glycolaldehyde + formaldehyde), from sugar photochemistry under cyanosulfidic conditions, or from reduction of pyruvaldehyde. Quantitative constraints on its steady-state concentration in surface environments are needed.\n\n3. **Threonine thermal stability**: Threonine is thermally labile and decomposes to glycine under hydrothermal conditions (Vallentyne, 1964; Bada et al., 1978). The balance between threonine production (via aldol condensation, Pathway 2) and thermal decomposition at hydrothermal temperatures is a key kinetic question. The retro-aldol decomposition of threonine to glycine (the reverse of the serine-type aldol reaction) may limit threonine accumulation in hydrothermal systems.\n\n4. **2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile stability**: Pathway 1 requires this aminonitrile intermediate to persist long enough for hydrolysis. Its stability under UV irradiation, varying pH, and temperature fluctuations in surface pools needs further study. Competing reactions (polymerization, reaction with other HCN-derived species) could reduce yields.\n\n5. **Formaldehyde concentration**: Pathway 2 requires formaldehyde, which is volatile and reactive. Its prebiotic concentration in hydrothermal environments is debated. Mineral adsorption, continuous production from CO2 reduction via serpentinization-derived H2, and thermophoretic concentration may maintain sufficient steady-state levels.\n\n6. **Alanine as precursor**: Pathway 2 requires L-alanine as a substrate, which must itself be produced by upstream prebiotic reactions. The availability of alanine in sufficient concentrations at hydrothermal vents, where it is simultaneously being consumed by aldol condensation and potentially decomposed at high temperatures, represents a kinetic bottleneck that needs quantitative modeling.\n\n7. **Selectivity and side products**: The aldol condensation of alanine with formaldehyde (Pathway 2) could potentially produce other isomeric products besides threonine. The stereoselectivity and regioselectivity of this reaction under prebiotic conditions, and the relative yields of threonine versus allo-threonine or other isomers, need further characterization.\n\n=== END CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHE